<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alsace-Lorraine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alsace-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> was the territory annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> in 1871. It was administered as an imperial territory and became a lasting source of French resentment. France recovered it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine.jpg" alt="Alsace-Lorraine" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawing created by David Liuzzo · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dienstflagge_Elsa%C3%9F-Lothringen_Kaiserreich.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Imperial Territory of Alsace-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> had a land area of 14,496 square kilometres and its capital was Strasbourg. It was divided into three districts: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-alsace">Upper Alsace</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> as its capital, corresponding to the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haut-rhin">Haut-Rhin</a>; Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, with Strasbourg, corresponding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bas-rhin">Bas-Rhin</a>; and Lorraine, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, corresponding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>. At the 1910 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the largest towns were Strasbourg with 220,883 inhabitants, Mulhouse with 128,190 and Metz with 102,787. Most inhabitants spoke German dialects, while French was spoken mainly in parts of Lorraine; in 1900 about 86.8 per cent gave a German or Germanic dialect as their first language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> progressively under Louis XIII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> in the 17th century, taking Strasbourg in 1681, and incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> from the 16th century to the 18th, the Duchy of Lorraine passing to the French crown on the death of Stanisław I Leszczyński in 1766. German nationalism, which arose in the 19th century as a reaction to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s occupation, pointed to the German dialects spoken in Alsace and northern Lorraine to justify including them in a future German state.</p><p>In 1871 the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> took the territory after its victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. The annexation was controversial even in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>: Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> was at first opposed, thinking it would create permanent French hostility, and some German industrialists feared competition from Mulhouse's cloth makers. The territory was ruled directly by the imperial government rather than as a state. A Territorial Committee with a consultative role was set up by decree of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> on 29 October 1874, and an imperial governor was introduced in 1879. The constitution of 31 May 1911 gave it an elected Landtag and three representatives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-german-empire">Bundesrat</a>. The French laws on religious bodies were kept, with public status for Catholic, Lutheran, Calvinist and Jewish bodies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>French resentment over the loss of the territory was one of the contributing factors to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took Alsace-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and the cession was confirmed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. During the Second World War Alsace and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> were annexed by Nazi Germany before being restored to France in 1944-45. Some legal provisions specific to the territory remain in force as the local law of Alsace-Moselle, and since 2016 the area has formed part of the French administrative region of Grand Est.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alsace–Lorraine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The duchy’s position between France and Germany foreshadowed the later contest over <b>Alsace-Lorraine</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Wilhelm I of Prussia was declared German Emperor, and the Treaty of Frankfurt that May annexed <b>Alsace-Lorraine</b> and imposed a five-billion-franc indemnity that helped fund early industrial expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeat by Germany in 1871 the return of <b>Alsace-Lorraine</b> drove French revanchism for a quarter century, and in the Dreyfus Affair of 1894–1906 conservative Catholic nationalists treated a Jewish officer as no true Frenchman.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
